## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to diagnose production rollouts with service meshes and canaries, focusing on end-to-end validation, live traffic checks, and safe rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Canary/rollout health in Istio
- Rollout status and events
- Readiness/Liveness probes relevance
- Safe rollback without downtime
- Synthetic traffic validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal helper to parse rollout JSON and extract canary status (pseudo, for illustration)
function getCanaryStatus(rolloutJson) {
  try {
    const r = JSON.parse(rolloutJson);
    return r?.status?.canary?.traffic || 0;
  } catch {
    return 0;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate the rollback decision with a health-check gate?
- What metrics would you collect to prove P95 stability during rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests deep production debugging skills, including multi-tenant cache management, Redis eviction policies, and safe rollout procedures under traffic.

## Key Concepts
- Redis eviction policies (volatile-lru, allkeys-lru, etc.) and TTL management
- Multi-tenant namespace isolation in Redis using key prefixes
- Canary rollout strategies (e.g., Argo Rollouts or manual phased deployments)
- Observability: Redis INFO metrics, hit/miss ratios, and application latency metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Verify current eviction policy
const evictionPolicy = await redis.configGet('maxmemory-policy');
console.log('Current eviction policy:', evictionPolicy);

// Check keyspace stats
const info = await redis.info('stats');
const hits = info.match(/keyspace_hits:(\d+)/)[1];
const misses = info.match(/keyspace_misses:(\d+)/)[1];
const hitRate = hits / (hits + misses);
console.log('Hit rate:', hitRate);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical data quality controls, schema evolution discipline, and safe deployment practices in multi-tenant streaming environments.

## Key Concepts
- Schema Registry with AVRO and compatibility enforcement
- Per-tenant schema namespaces and semantic versioning
- Real-time drift detection in Flink processing pipeline
- Automated quarantine and alerting mechanisms
- Circuit breaker patterns for data quality protection

## Code Example
```java
// Flink schema validation with drift detection
public class SchemaValidator extends ProcessFunction<GenericRecord, GenericRecord> {
    private final SchemaRegistryClient registry;

    @Override
    public void processElement(GenericRecord record, Context ctx) {
        try {
            Schema schema = registry.getLatestSchema(tenantTopic);
            if (!isValidAgainstSchema(record, schema)) {
                ctx.output(quarantineTag, record);
                alertDashboard("SCHEMA_DRIFT", tenantTopic);
            } else {
                ctx.output(validTag, record);
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            ctx.output(errorTag, record);
        }
    }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle non-breaking field removals while maintaining data lake query compatibility?
- How would you scale drift checks for high-throughput topics (>100K msg/sec)?
- What's your approach for schema evolution across multiple downstream consumers?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design scalable feature flags with per-user rollout, regional controls, and safe hot flips.

## Key Concepts
- Global distribution and eventual consistency
- Data modeling for flags and segments
- Cache invalidation and TTL
- Rollout semantics and hot-flip safety

## Code Example
```javascript
function evaluateFeature(user, flag, context){
  const bucket = hash(user.id + flag.name) % 100;
  const inSegment = (flag.segments && flag.segments.length) ? flag.segments.includes(user.segment) : true;
  const regionOK = flag.regionOverride ? user.region === flag.regionOverride : true;
  return (bucket < flag.rolloutPct) && inSegment && regionOK;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate rollout accuracy under traffic spikes?
- How would you rollback a faulty flag without service interruption?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical rate-limiting basics, a common reliability concern in public APIs. It tests per-key quotas, in-memory state, and how small designs behave under restart and burst scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Sliding window algorithm
- Map-based per-key state
- Time-based pruning
- Testing strategy and limitations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Core limiter (simplified)
const limiter = new Map();

function allowRequest(key, now = Date.now()) {
  const windowMs = 60000;
  const limit = 60;
  const timestamps = limiter.get(key) || [];
  while (timestamps.length && timestamps[0] <= now - windowMs) timestamps.shift();
  if (timestamps.length < limit) {
    timestamps.push(now);
    limiter.set(key, timestamps);
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for multiple instances?
- How would you test time-dependent behavior without slow sleeps?
